LUPUS = C("lupus", "Lupus", "🦋",
    "Free lupus (SLE) quiz: 12 questions on the butterfly rash, ANA, hydroxychloroquine, kidneys, pregnancy and sun protection.",
    "Twelve questions, easy to hard, on systemic lupus erythematosus: the many ways it shows up, the tests used, and the treatments that keep it quiet.",
    [
        Q("What kind of disease is lupus?", "An autoimmune disease", ["An infection", "A cancer", "An allergy"],
          "The immune system attacks the body’s own tissues: skin, joints, kidneys, blood and more."),
        Q("Who is most often affected?", "Women aged 15 to 45", ["Men over 80", "Babies and toddlers", "Only elite athletes"],
          "About 9 in 10 people with lupus are women. It’s more common in Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander and Asian people."),
        Q("Which rash is classic for lupus?", "A butterfly rash across the cheeks", ["A rash on the soles only", "Silvery plaques on the elbows", "A target-shaped rash"],
          "It often flares with sun. Many people with lupus never get it."),
        Q("Which blood test is positive in nearly all people with lupus?", "ANA (antinuclear antibody)", ["Rheumatoid factor", "HbA1c", "Ferritin"],
          "A negative ANA makes lupus very unlikely. But a positive ANA is common in healthy people, so it doesn’t prove lupus."),
        Q("Which test is more specific for lupus?", "Anti-double-stranded DNA (anti-dsDNA)", ["CRP", "Vitamin D", "TSH"],
          "Anti-dsDNA and anti-Smith antibodies are much more specific. Anti-dsDNA levels can track kidney activity."),
        Q("Which medicine is recommended for almost everyone with lupus?", "Hydroxychloroquine", ["Allopurinol", "Warfarin for all", "Antibiotics"],
          "It reduces flares and organ damage. Yearly eye checks are needed after the first five years (sooner with risk factors)."),
        Q("Why does sun protection matter?", "UV light can trigger rashes and flares", ["Sun causes lupus in everyone", "It doesn't matter in lupus", "Lupus makes skin immune to sunburn"],
          "SPF 50+, hats and covering up are part of treatment."),
        Q("Which organ involvement needs regular urine checks?", "The kidneys (lupus nephritis)", ["The appendix (lupus appendicitis)", "The gallbladder (lupus cholecystitis)", "The tonsils (lupus tonsillitis)"],
          "Protein or blood in the urine can be the first sign. Early treatment protects kidney function."),
        Q("What do antiphospholipid antibodies increase the risk of?", "Blood clots and miscarriage", ["Bleeding gums only", "Hair growth", "High blood sugar"],
          "People with these antibodies may need blood thinners, especially in pregnancy."),
        Q("Which blood count finding is common in active lupus?", "Low white cells or platelets", ["Very high platelets", "High haemoglobin", "High eosinophils"],
          "Low counts and low complement (C3 and C4) often signal activity."),
        Q("What is drug-induced lupus?", "A lupus-like illness from a medicine; settles when stopped", ["Lupus caused by antibiotics, in everyone who takes them", "A permanent, more severe form of lupus", "Lupus that is being treated with medicines"],
          "Classic causes include hydralazine, procainamide and some TNF inhibitors."),
        Q("Why plan pregnancy with the lupus team?", "6 months in remission first lowers risks", ["Pregnancy is impossible with lupus", "Lupus always worsens in pregnancy", "All medicines must stop beforehand"],
          "Hydroxychloroquine is usually continued. Some medicines, such as mycophenolate, must be stopped well before."),
    ],
    [("Is lupus contagious?", "No. It’s an autoimmune disease, not an infection."),
     ("Can lupus be cured?", "Not yet, but most people live full lives with treatment and regular monitoring."),
     ("Is this quiz medical advice?", "No. It’s general education. See your GP or rheumatologist about your own care.")])

SARC = C("sarcoidosis", "Sarcoidosis", "🫁",
    "Free sarcoidosis quiz: 12 questions on granulomas, chest X-ray stages, Löfgren syndrome, eyes, heart, calcium and treatment.",
    "Twelve questions, easy to hard, on sarcoidosis: what granulomas are, where they show up and who needs treatment.",
    [
        Q("What forms in sarcoidosis?", "Granulomas: small clumps of inflammatory cells", ["Cancerous tumours", "Small blood clots", "Kidney stones only"],
          "They can form in any organ but most often the lungs and lymph nodes."),
        Q("Which organ is affected in about 9 in 10 people?", "The lungs and chest lymph nodes", ["The appendix", "The spleen alone", "The bladder"],
          "Often found on a chest X-ray done for another reason."),
        Q("What is the classic chest X-ray finding?", "Swollen lymph nodes at both lung roots", ["A collapsed lung on one side", "Fluid around the heart", "A single large lung mass"],
          "Called bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy."),
        Q("What is Löfgren syndrome?", "Fever, red shin lumps, sore ankles, swollen chest nodes", ["Sarcoidosis affecting the brain and nerves", "A drug reaction to sarcoidosis treatment", "A form of lung cancer that mimics sarcoid"],
          "It usually settles on its own within months."),
        Q("Which eye problem can sarcoidosis cause?", "Uveitis (inflammation inside the eye)", ["Short-sightedness", "Colour blindness", "Cataracts from birth"],
          "Red, painful eyes or blurred vision need urgent review. Yearly eye checks are recommended."),
        Q("Which blood level can be high in sarcoidosis?", "Calcium", ["Potassium", "Haemoglobin", "Platelets"],
          "Granulomas make active vitamin D, which raises calcium. Avoid high-dose vitamin D unless your team advises it."),
        Q("Which blood test is often raised but not diagnostic?", "ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme)", ["Troponin", "Lipase", "PSA"],
          "It’s non-specific, so the diagnosis rests on symptoms, imaging and biopsy."),
        Q("How is the diagnosis usually confirmed?", "A biopsy showing non-caseating granulomas", ["A stool test", "A urine dipstick", "A skin prick test"],
          "Other causes of granulomas, such as TB and fungal infection, must be excluded."),
        Q("Does everyone with sarcoidosis need treatment?", "No. Many improve on their own", ["Yes, always lifelong", "Yes, with surgery", "Yes, with antibiotics"],
          "Treatment is for symptoms or risk to organs such as eyes, heart, nerves or kidneys."),
        Q("What is the usual first treatment when needed?", "Prednisolone", ["Insulin", "Amoxicillin", "Aspirin alone"],
          "Methotrexate or other agents may be added to reduce the steroid dose."),
        Q("Why does cardiac sarcoidosis matter?", "It can cause heart block and dangerous rhythms", ["It only causes chest itch", "It's always harmless", "It only lowers blood pressure"],
          "Palpitations, fainting or ECG changes need cardiac MRI or PET scanning."),
        Q("Which skin lesion on the nose and cheeks is linked to chronic sarcoidosis?", "Lupus pernio", ["Acne rosacea", "Port-wine stain", "Cold sores"],
          "Purple, swollen plaques on the face. It suggests long-lasting disease."),
    ],
    [("Is sarcoidosis cancer?", "No. Granulomas are inflammation, not cancer."),
     ("Is it contagious?", "No. The cause is unknown, but it doesn’t spread between people."),
     ("Is this quiz medical advice?", "No. It’s general education. See your GP or specialist about your own care.")])

